#ifndef SKY_LOOK_GLSLH
#define SKY_LOOK_GLSLH

// THE ONE PLACE AN HDR SKY'S AUTHORED LOOK IS APPLIED — at the SAMPLE, by every reader of the sky.
//
// The environment cubes of an `.hdr` skybox are baked ONCE, in the panorama's own orientation at unit
// gain (EnvironmentManager::Create). The scene's rotation, intensity and tint turn and scale the LOOKUP
// instead, which is what lets a slider drag cost a uniform write rather than a device-idling rebake per
// value. It is exact, not an approximation: a rotation about the up axis commutes with both
// convolutions (the irradiance at R*n of the unturned sky is the irradiance at n of the turned one, and
// the GGX lobe is rotation-invariant in the same way), and the gain is linear.
//
// WHO MUST CALL THIS, and what keeps them honest. Every program that declares an environment cube —
// the skybox pass, the deferred composite, the forward PBR variants, the glass, the shader-graph surface
// and the editor's cubemap preview — declares `SkyLookUB` and reads through these two functions. The
// bake used to apply the look precisely so that no consumer could miss it; the census in
// Desert/Tests/Engine/SkyPanorama now makes missing it a red test instead.
//
// The includer declares the block itself, at a slot free in ITS layout (the layouts differ, and the
// DShader parser's occupancy scan reads literal digits only, so a macro slot would be invisible to it):
//
//     Uniform(N) SkyLookUB
//     {
//         vec4 YawCosSin; // xy = (cos yaw, sin yaw) — Graphic::SkyLookGPU
//         vec4 Gain;      // rgb = tint * intensity
//     } skyLook;
//
// This text declares nothing, so it also compiles as C++ (Desert/Tests/Engine/SkyPanorama).

// The cube direction a world direction reads, with the sky's authored yaw applied.
//
// THE ROTATION IS APPLIED TO THE LOOKUP, BY MINUS THE ANGLE, so the IMAGE turns by plus the angle: an
// author who types 90 wants the sun that was in the east to end up in the north, not the camera to have
// turned under a fixed sky. Written out rather than built as a mat3 because this text also compiles as
// C++, where a mat3 literal's column/row order is one more thing to get wrong.
vec3 SkyLookDirection(vec3 direction, vec2 yawCosSin)
{
    return vec3( yawCosSin.x * direction.x - yawCosSin.y * direction.z,
                 direction.y,
                 yawCosSin.x * direction.z + yawCosSin.y * direction.x );
}

// The authored brightness and colour of the sky, applied to radiance read out of an environment cube.
// ONE LINE, AND A FUNCTION ON PURPOSE: a bare multiply is the kind of thing that gets added to some
// readers and not others; a named call is something the source census can require of all of them.
vec3 ApplySkyGain(vec3 radiance, vec3 gain)
{
    return radiance * gain;
}

#endif // SKY_LOOK_GLSLH
